tairwell.\"","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm working out whether, under A-FDT as the essay interprets it, the two copies with different observations subjunctively depend on each other. The other copy computes the same function on a different input, and A-FDT treats that as a separate variable with its own prior, so I'm weighing whether that independence holds in this prisoner's dilemma with symmetric payoffs.\n\nUnder the fixed-point reading with separate nodes for different inputs, defecting dominates and the consistent outcome is defect. I'm also weighing a mixture version, where the other copy matches my output with some credence s, and checking whether quieting beats submitting once s is large enough.\n\nI'm comparing quieting against submitting under the mixture: with the other copy defecting by fixed point, quieting wins only if s exceeds 1/3. Then I'm settling the judgment call. The essay's own treatment of differently-input copies as separate nodes means my choice doesn't causally reach the other's action, so submitting dominates, and I'll submit with a short explanation.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working out whether, under A-FDT as the essay interprets it, the two copies with different observations subjunctively depend on each other. The other copy computes the same function on a different input, and A-FDT treats that as a separate variable with its own prior, so I'm weighing whether that independence holds in this prisoner's dilemma with symmetric payoffs.\n\nUnder the fixed-point reading with separate nodes for different inputs, defecting dominates and the consistent outcome is defect. I'm also weighing a mixture version, where the other copy matches my output with some credence s, and checking whether quieting beats submitting once s is large enough.\n\nI'm comparing quieting against submitting under the mixture: with the other copy defecting by fixed point, quieting wins only if s exceeds 1/3. Then I'm settling the judgment call. The essay's own treatment of differently-input copies as separate nodes means my choice doesn't causally reach the other's action, so submitting dominates, and I'll submit with a short explanation.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":9432,"completion_tokens":1397,"total_tokens":10829,"cost":0.065668,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.065668,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.037728,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.02794},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":817,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}